#footnote(numbering: "*")[Corresponding author: Marc Christ, #link("mailto:marc.christ@fbh-berlin.de"), #link("https://www.fbh-berlin.de")]

#align(center)[*ABSTRACT*]

Transferring atomic quantum sensors from laboratory-scale physics packages to compact, alignment-free sensor heads suitable for field and space deployment requires a structural platform that combines mechanical, thermal, and optical functionality within a single, manufacturable component. We present such a platform based on additively manufactured technical ceramics: lithography-based ceramic manufacturing is used to fabricate monolithic micro-optical benches with application-specific mounting geometries, while high-precision active alignment and adhesive bonding realize long-term stable optical assemblies through micro-integration. As a first system-level demonstrator, we present a micro-integrated optical frequency reference based on Doppler-free spectroscopy of the rubidium D#sub[2] line, integrated within a 6~mL Al#sub[2]O#sub[3] module. A laser stabilized to the reference reaches a fractional frequency instability of $3 times 10^(- 12)$ at 1~s. We extend the platform to a compact optically pumped magnetometer, exploiting the magnetic cleanliness and tailored thermal properties of high-purity ceramics for a 7~mL sensor head with picotesla sensitivity aimed at space-borne biomagnetometry, and to a micro-integrated optical dipole trap with active MEMS-mirror beam steering for cold-atom manipulation. Together, these demonstrators show that ceramic additive manufacturing and optical micro-integration form a versatile, robust platform for compact atomic sensor systems in field and space applications.

*Keywords:* atomic quantum sensors, additive manufacturing, technical ceramics, micro-integration, miniaturized optical systems, optical frequency reference, optically pumped magnetometer, atom trapping

= Introduction
<sec:introduction>
Atom-based quantum sensors exploit well-defined atomic states and
transitions to measure physical quantities such as magnetic and electric
fields, gravity, and inertial forces with high sensitivity and accuracy,
or to serve as frequency references in precision timing systems
#super[#(link(<bib-Kitching2011>)[1] + [, ] + link(<bib-Bongs2019>)[2] + [, ] + link(<bib-Degen2017>)[3])]. Their applications span precision
metrology and navigation, resource exploration, and fundamental physics.
In space, such systems are considered for next-generation timing and
navigation infrastructure #super[#(link(<bib-Schuldt2021>)[4])], with optical frequency
references already demonstrated in sounding-rocket experiments
#super[#(link(<bib-Dinkelaker2017>)[5] + [, ] + link(<bib-Doeringshoff2019>)[6])] and optical atomic clocks currently
under development for in-orbit demonstration #super[#(link(<bib-Kuschewski2024>)[7])]. Cold-atom
sensors are further investigated for future satellite-based Earth
observation missions #super[#(link(<bib-Leveque2022>)[8])]. Atomic magnetometers offer access to
weak biomagnetic signals, such as magnetic fields generated by brain or
muscle activity, providing a route towards non-invasive medical
diagnostics without cryogenic sensor operation
#super[#(link(<bib-Boto2018>)[9] + [, ] + link(<bib-Tierney2019>)[10] + [, ] + link(<bib-Aslam2023>)[11])]. As discussed in #link(<sec:opm>)[4],
this capability is of particular interest for astronaut health
monitoring on crewed space missions. These examples illustrate the broad
potential of atomic quantum systems beyond specialized laboratory
environments.

Realizing this potential in practical field and space applications,
however, requires a substantial reduction in size, weight, power
consumption, and system complexity. The sensor's core subsystem,
commonly referred to as the physics package, typically combines an
optical system for atomic state preparation, manipulation, and detection
with the atomic ensemble, contained in a vapor cell or vacuum system.
Additional subsystems tailored to the specific sensing modality, such as
field generation or thermal control, are integrated alongside these core
components. Because sensor performance is highly sensitive to optical
alignment, the physics package must remain mechanically and thermally
stable under environmental loads. Conventional laboratory
implementations rely on adjustable optomechanics and comparatively long
free-space beam paths. While highly flexible during development, such
assemblies require expert alignment and maintenance and must instead be
transferred into compact, alignment-free subsystems and sensor heads
suitable for mobile deployment.

Micro-integration provides an established route towards compact and
mechanically stable optical assemblies. Electro-optical components with
characteristic dimensions of only a few millimeters can be actively
aligned and bonded onto a micro-optical bench (MIOB), resulting in
compact systems that no longer require adjustable optomechanics after
assembly #super[#(link(<bib-Schiemangk2015>)[12] + [, ] + link(<bib-Kuerbis2020>)[13] + [, ] + link(<bib-Christ2024a>)[14])]. Related integration
technologies have been successfully employed in drop-tower #super[#(link(<bib-Muntinga2013>)[15])]
and sounding-rocket experiments
#super[#(link(<bib-Dinkelaker2017>)[5] + [, ] + link(<bib-Becker2018a>)[16] + [, ] + link(<bib-Doeringshoff2019>)[6])] and are planned for further
space missions #super[#(link(<bib-Frye2021>)[17])] aboard the ISS. For application in such
demanding environments, the optical integration process must be
reproducible and robust. Adhesive bonding technologies used for
micro-integration have therefore been subjected to dedicated
environmental qualification campaigns #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2023>)[18])].

The MIOB is not only a mechanical carrier but a central, functional part
of the optical system, integrating the various mechanical, optical,
thermal, and electrical features of the physics package. To keep
component placement and optical alignment stable under mechanical and
thermal loads, its material must combine high stiffness, low mass, and a
low coefficient of thermal expansion (CTE). Technical ceramics are
particularly attractive for this purpose because they combine high
stiffness and temperature stability with low density, electrical
insulation, vacuum compatibility, magnetic cleanliness, and a broad
range of available thermal properties. These characteristics are
relevant for robust optical and electronic assemblies and make technical
ceramics an interesting material class for compact field- and
space-oriented sensor systems. Detailed material properties and a
comparison with other materials commonly used in compact quantum systems
are given in Ref.~#link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19].

Additive manufacturing (AM) complements these material properties with a
high degree of geometric freedom, overcoming key limitations of
traditional ceramic manufacturing such as restricted design complexity,
high tooling costs, and long production times. Because it avoids
per-design tooling, AM is particularly cost-effective for complex
geometries across a wide range of production volumes, from single,
highly customized components such as space instrumentation to larger
production series. Complex three-dimensional mounting structures,
internal cavities, lightweight structures, and application-specific
interfaces can be incorporated directly and efficiently into a
monolithic ceramic part. This geometric freedom further enables topology
optimization approaches, supporting lightweight designs, tailored
thermal properties, and material savings. In previous work, we
introduced lithography-based ceramic manufacturing (LCM) for compact
quantum technologies and demonstrated its use for a micro-integrated
optical frequency reference #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19])]. Here, we present the ceramic AM
and micro-integration approach as a broader technology platform for
compact optical and atomic sensor systems. After introducing the
manufacturing and integration concept, we discuss the optical frequency
reference CerAMRef as an integrated demonstrator and subsequently extend
the approach to compact optically pumped magnetometers and optical
systems with integrated active beam-steering elements for cold-atom
manipulation.

= Ceramic additive manufacturing and micro-integration platform
<sec:ceramic_am>
== Technical ceramics and lithography-based ceramic manufacturing
<subsec:lcm>
Additive manufacturing has increasingly been investigated for components
of compact quantum systems, including metallic vacuum parts, magnetic
coils and shields, and polymer structures for mounting optical
components #super[#(link(<bib-Norrgard2018a>)[20] + [, ] + link(<bib-Saint2018>)[21] + [, ] + link(<bib-Vovrosh2017>)[22] + [, ] + link(<bib-Madkhaly2022>)[23])]. Technical
ceramics complement these printable material classes with properties
especially advantageous for precise optical and atomic sensor assemblies
deployed on mobile platforms or in space. LCM enables printing of
various oxide and nitride ceramics, including alumina (Al#sub[2]O#sub[3]), zirconia (ZrO#sub[2]),
aluminum nitride (AlN), and silicon nitride (SiN), with mechanical and thermal
properties comparable to traditionally manufactured ceramics: Al#sub[2]O#sub[3] combines
high stiffness with moderate density, electrical insulation, temperature
stability, and vacuum compatibility, while ZrO#sub[2] and AlN offer, respectively,
thermal isolation and high thermal conductivity for applications
requiring substantially different thermal behavior. A detailed
comparison of relevant properties for additively manufactured materials
is provided in Ref.~#link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19]. Ceramic components for the presented physics
packages in this work are fabricated by LCM using a CeraFab S65 system
(Lithoz GmbH): a vat-photopolymerization process in which a suspension
of ceramic particles in a photosensitive binder is selectively
polymerized layer by layer using a digital micromirror device
#super[#(link(<bib-Schwentenwein2014>)[24] + [, ] + link(<bib-Schwentenwein2015>)[25])]. The printer provides a lateral
discretization of 40~μm over a build area of $102 times 64$~mm and supports layer
thicknesses between 10~μm and 100~μm; a layer thickness of 25~μm is
typically used for the MIOBs discussed here #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19])].

After printing, uncured slurry is cleaned off the parts and the
resulting green parts, which consist of ceramic particles held together
by the cured polymer binder, are thermally processed: the binder is
removed during debinding before the ceramic is densified by sintering,
with the CAD geometry scaled prior to printing to compensate for the
resulting material-specific shrinkage. For oxide ceramics, including Al#sub[2]O#sub[3] and ZrO#sub[2], the complete process chain from printing and cleaning through
debinding and sintering is operated in-house, enabling rapid iteration
from component design, fabrication, and geometric inspection to system
assembly. #link(<fig:lcm_platform>)[1]\(a) shows printed parts on the
build platform, while #link(<fig:lcm_platform>)[1]\(b) shows
representative MIOBs after complete processing. In addition to Al#sub[2]O#sub[3]
structures, ZrO#sub[2] has been introduced for applications in which thermal
isolation is an integral part of the mechanical design. Examples include
compact vapor-cell ovens and thermally insulating MIOBs. In this way,
the ceramic material is selected to match the specific requirements of
each application.

