== Design for optical micro-integration
<subsec:microintegration>
A major advantage of ceramic AM for optical systems is the ability to
combine the mechanical base structure and component-specific mounting
geometries within a monolithic part. Instead of using a planar substrate
with separately manufactured mounts, features such as V-grooves,
pockets, alignment edges, recesses, sidewalls, cable or fiber guides,
and interfaces for sensors can be incorporated directly into the optical
bench, allowing irregular components such as glass-blown atomic vapor
cells to be accommodated by geometries adapted to their actual
dimensions. These printed features provide mounting surfaces and, in
some cases, mechanical pre-alignment for the optical components,
together with reference geometries that define the beam path layout. The
final component positions are subsequently established during
micro-integration on the respective mounting surface. Thus the
dimensional precision of LCM itself does not need to provide a final
optical alignment accuracy. For the previously characterized Al#sub[2]O#sub[3] optical
benches, dimensional deviations of relevant features remained on the
order of several tens of micrometers #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19])], sufficient to position
millimeter-scale optical components well within the range of the
subsequent active alignment process.

Micro-integration is performed using precision positioning systems that
manipulate up to four optical and/or electro-optical components
simultaneously in all six degrees of freedom, with actuator step sizes
down to 1~nm and 1~μrad #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2023>)[18] + [, ] + link(<bib-Christ2024a>)[14])]. The optical subsystem
is operated during assembly and the relevant optical signal is used
directly as the alignment criterion. Aligned components are subsequently
fixed to the ceramic substrate by thermal- or UV-curing adhesive
bonding, qualified for micro-integrated optical systems under
environmental and vacuum requirements #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2023>)[18])]. The bonding
permanently transfers the optical alignment into a mechanically stable
assembly without adjustable mounts. This combination of
application-specific ceramic geometries, active optical alignment, and
qualified bonding has already been demonstrated for direct cold-atom
manipulation in a micro-integrated crossed-beam optical dipole trap on a
ceramic MIOB, showing long-term alignment stability and robustness under
operational conditions #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2024a>)[14])].

= Micro-integrated optical frequency reference
<sec:ceramref>
As a first system-level demonstrator combining ceramic AM and optical
micro-integration, we developed a micro-integrated optical frequency
reference, referred to as CerAMRef, based on Doppler-free spectroscopy
of the rubidium D#sub[2] line at 780~nm #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19])]. Such references are required
for stabilizing laser sources used in atomic-physics experiments and
quantum sensors and are conventionally realized using free-space
spectroscopy setups occupying substantially larger volumes.

The CerAMRef implements a Doppler-free retro-reflection spectroscopy
geometry in a compact, fiber-coupled layout with a volume of
approximately 6~mL, shown in #link(<fig:ceramref>)[2]\(a). Light enters
the module through a fiber collimator with a polarization-maintaining
fiber and passes a polarizing beam splitter (PBS) before propagating
through a rubidium vapor cell. A quarter-wave plate and dielectric
mirror generate a counter-propagating second pass through the cell.
After returning through the quarter-wave plate, the orthogonally
polarized light is separated at the polarizing beam splitter and focused
onto a photodiode. The resulting counter-propagating geometry enables
Doppler-free spectroscopy while maintaining a short and compact optical
layout. Further details of the spectroscopy configuration are given in
Ref.~#link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19].

#figure(image("ceramref_adev.svg"),
