  ]
)
<fig:ceramref>

The optical system is integrated on a monolithic LCM-fabricated Al#sub[2]O#sub[3] MIOB.
Custom mounting geometries are incorporated directly into the ceramic
substrate for the fiber collimator, micro-optics, photodiode,
temperature sensor, and the glass-blown rubidium cell. In particular,
the geometry surrounding the vapor cell illustrates an advantage of
additive manufacturing: the mounting structure can be adapted to the
non-ideal geometry of an individual glass component while simultaneously
defining its position relative to the optical axis. Most free-space
optical components have characteristic edge lengths of approximately
2.5~mm. They are actively aligned while monitoring the optical signal
and subsequently bonded to the ceramic substrate using the qualified
micro-integration approach described in Ref.~#link(<bib-Christ2023>)[18]. After bonding, the system
forms an alignment-free optical assembly without adjustable
optomechanics.

#link(<fig:ceramref>)[2]\(a) shows the assembled module with its ceramic
lid removed. The compact arrangement of the vapor cell, micro-optics,
detector, and fiber interface illustrates the high degree of integration
enabled by the printed three-dimensional substrate. A Pt100 temperature
sensor is incorporated into the MIOB and the overall module is
temperature stabilized using a Peltier element. A compact PCB provides
the electrical interface and photodiode amplification. The ceramic
housing has outer dimensions of approximately
14.8~mm$times$40~mm$times$9.5~mm. The complete integrated spectroscopy
module has a mass of 15~g, including the optical components. An optical
path length of approximately 53~mm is realized inside the module. The
fiber-coupled architecture allows the frequency reference to be
integrated as an independent subsystem into higher-level systems.

The performance of the CerAMRef was evaluated by stabilizing a
narrow-linewidth external-cavity diode laser (ECDL) to the #super[87]Rb D#sub[2]
$F = 2 arrow.r F' = 2 \/ 3$ crossover transition of the Doppler-free
rubidium spectrum using frequency-modulation spectroscopy (FMS), which
provides a suitable dispersive signal for laser stabilization. The
employed micro-integrated ECDL is based on a compact laser technology
platform developed for robust field and space-oriented optical systems
#super[#(link(<bib-Kuerbis2020>)[13])]. The frequency instability of the stabilized system was
determined from a beat-note measurement against an independent rubidium
reference based on modulation-transfer spectroscopy. The complete
experimental configuration, including the optical-power stabilization,
modulation electronics, and frequency evaluation procedure, is described
in detail in Ref.~#link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19]; only the resulting frequency-reference performance
is summarized here.

#link(<fig:ceramref>)[2]\(b) shows the overlapping Allan deviation of
the beat-note frequency. The measured fractional frequency instability
reaches $3 times 10^(- 12)$ at an averaging time of 1~s and
approximately $4 times 10^(- 12)$ at 100~s. The instability remains
below $1 times 10^(- 11)$ for averaging times up to approximately 300~s
and below $1 times 10^(- 10)$ up to 1~×~10#super[4]~s #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2024>)[19])].

These results are typical for a rubidium D#sub[2]-line FMS reference, yet
achieved within a 6~mL module---a volume reduction of multiple orders of
magnitude compared to conventional free-space implementations. This
highlights how the ceramic AM platform facilitates the integration of
highly compact, mechanically stable, alignment-free systems, while
retaining performance sufficient for laser-frequency stabilization in
cold-atom experiments and related atomic quantum sensors. The CerAMRef
therefore demonstrates the central concept of the ceramic integration
platform: the additively manufactured component serves as a highly
stable functionalized substrate that provides the mounting and reference
geometries for a complete optical subsystem, with the final optical
alignment established through precision micro-integration.

= Compact optically pumped magnetometer
<sec:opm>
Optically Pumped Magnetometers (OPMs) exploit the quantum mechanical
interaction between atomic spins and optical fields to achieve highly
sensitive magnetic field measurements #super[#(link(<bib-Budker2007>)[26])]. In a typical alkali
vapor OPM setup, resonant circularly polarized laser light is
transmitted through an atomic vapor cell, transferring angular momentum
from photons to valence electrons. This optical pumping process drives
the atomic ensemble into a coherent, spin-polarized ground state. In the
presence of external magnetic fields, the macroscopically polarized
atomic spins undergo Larmor precession around the magnetic field vector
at a frequency proportional to the local field strength. This spin
precession modulates the optical absorption and dispersion properties of
the atomic medium at the Larmor frequency. By detecting the transmitted
optical power or polarization components, minute magnetic field changes
can be extracted with sub-pT precision. Crucially, this high-resolution
measurement is achieved without requiring the cryogenic cooling
infrastructure necessary for Superconducting Quantum Interference
Devices (SQUIDs), making OPMs inherently more suitable for mobile
applications and remote deployment #super[#(link(<bib-Tiporlini2013>)[27])].

For OPM operation, the magnetic cleanliness of high-purity ceramics is
of particular relevance: their near-zero magnetic footprint, resulting
from low magnetic susceptibility, avoids local structural magnetic bias
and stray field contamination that would otherwise interfere with
high-sensitivity measurements. A tailored thermal design, combining
application-optimized geometries with a suitably selected ceramic
material, further enables stable operation at reduced power consumption,
supporting compact, skin-safe sensor head concepts for wearable or
body-mounted deployment.

The physics package of our $M_x$-type OPM in development, depicted in
#link(<fig:OPM>)[3], occupies a volume of approximately 7~mL
with a total mass of 16~g and is integrated onto a ceramic MIOB.
Fiber-coupled light delivery keeps the light source separate from the
sensing unit, preserving magnetic cleanliness. After collimation and
polarization cleaning, the circularly polarized light is transmitted
through a heated cesium vapor cell. In ongoing development, the cell
oven is thermally isolated using additively manufactured ZrO#sub[2] structures,
reducing the stand-off distance required between the optical sensing
volume and the sample under investigation compared to conventional
thermal insulation approaches; the next-generation ZrO#sub[2] bench for this
demonstrator is shown in #link(<fig:lcm_platform>)[1]\(b). The cell is
sandwiched between a pair of radio-frequency Helmholtz coils that
coherently drive the spin resonance. Finally, the transmitted light is
detected on a photodiode.

#side-figure(image("source/figures/OPM_grey_cropped.png", width: 100%), 70%)[Miniaturized $M_x$-type OPM sensor head, occupying a volume of approximately 7~mL. The core of the demonstrator unit is a monolithic MIOB additively manufactured from alumina (Al#sub[2]O#sub[3]).] <fig:OPM>


The primary application driving our OPM developments is non-invasive
space-borne biomagnetometry. Extended exposure to microgravity during
crewed space missions causes degeneration of the neuromuscular
apparatus. Tracking muscle atrophy and evaluating countermeasure
exercise routines inside spacecraft habitats requires continuous
contactless diagnostic tools. Resolving such biomagnetic fields via
magnetomyography (MMG) demands magnetic field probes capable of sub-pT
sensitivities across measurement bandwidths up to the kHz range
#super[#(link(<bib-Semeia22>)[28])]. First measurements with our OPM demonstrators show that they
are capable of detecting magnetic fields with a sensitivity of
1.2~pT/$sqrt(upright("Hz"))$ at a bandwidth of 260~Hz.

Beyond space health monitoring and diagnostics, miniaturized
magnetometers have strong potential for broader space exploration and
terrestrial sensing missions. In GNSS-denied environments, such as
terrestrial settings like urban canyons and underground infrastructure,
or potentially on lunar or Martian surfaces, Magnetic-Aided Inertial
Navigation Systems (MAINS) utilize real-time magnetic field measurements
to match local crustal magnetic anomaly maps, constraining accelerometer
drift in autonomous landers, rovers, and ground vehicles
#super[#(link(<bib-Huang24>)[29] + [, ] + link(<bib-Ingleby24>)[30])].

= Integration of active optical elements for atom trapping
<sec:active_optics>
Cold-atom-based sensor concepts benefit from dynamic control of the
optical trapping potential, e.g., by rapidly scanning a focused beam to
create a time-averaged, or "painted," potential, which provides trap
geometries and evaporation efficiencies not accessible with static
optical dipole traps #super[#(link(<bib-Henderson_2009>)[31])]. Classical beam-steering approaches
for such applications, such as acousto-optic deflectors #super[#(link(<bib-Barredo2016>)[32])] or
digital micromirror devices #super[#(link(<bib-Stuart2017>)[33])], are effective but comparatively
bulky and power-hungry, limiting their suitability for miniaturized,
integrated optical systems. Building on our previously demonstrated
static crossed-beam optical dipole trap on a ceramic MIOB #super[#(link(<bib-Christ2024a>)[14])],
we extend the platform to active optical elements by integrating a
compact piezoelectric MEMS mirror for dynamic beam steering onto the
ceramic bench, see #link(<fig:MEMSodt>)[4].

The optical setup is designed around an additively manufactured Al#sub[2]O#sub[3] MIOB
with a compact footprint of $42 times 31.5$~mm, occupying a volume of 21~mL with a mass of
19~g. It features multiple alignment markers and mounting features for
the optical components. The additive manufacturing process enables the
integration of a gyroid lattice structure, reducing the substrate's mass
while ensuring high mechanical rigidity and minimizing excess material.
Furthermore, rapid prototyping enabled the iterative optimization of the
mechanical alignment and mounting features, which are tailored to the
specifications of the MEMS mirror used. The mirror is adhesively bonded
onto the ceramic bench and connected to a PCB, which facilitates
wire-bonded electrical connections for its operation.

A pigtailed fiber collimator delivers polarized laser light onto the
bench depicted in #link(<fig:MEMSodt>)[4]. The light is
further conditioned by a PBS and reflected onto a gold-coated MEMS
mirror manufactured by Fraunhofer ISIT. The actively steered laser beam
is subsequently guided through a focusing lens, which translates the
steering angle into a lateral beam displacement by up to ~±~1~mm for
this design. As a result, the beam focus
($w_(upright(0)) approx 30 med upright("μ") upright("m")$) creating the
dipole trap behind the optical system is dynamically displaced in its
focal plane. The 2D MEMS mirror enables either one-dimensional painting
along the main axes of the focal plane or combined two-dimensional
painting following a Lissajous pattern, depending on driving
frequencies, which are in the range of about 3~kHz--9~kHz.

#side-figure(image("source/figures/mems_odt_image.JPG", width: 100%), 55%)[Miniaturized active optical dipole trap system (volume: 21~mL, mass: 19~g) employing a 2D MEMS mirror for dynamic beam steering. The MIOB integrates a mass-reducing gyroid lattice structure alongside customized alignment and mounting features for the optical components.] <fig:MEMSodt>


The system is designed to operate at optical powers up to 5~W continuous
wave (cw) at 1064~nm in a cold-atom experiment, where it is being
evaluated for improved loading of #super[87]Rb atoms from a magneto-optical trap into
the optical dipole trap. Further applications of the technology include
compact ultra-cold-atom systems, creating Bose-Einstein condensates
(BEC), which could enable long-lived quantum memories in space
#super[#(link(<bib-DaRos2023>)[34])].

= Conclusion and outlook
<sec:conclusion>
Additively manufactured technical ceramics provide a versatile
structural platform for the integration of compact optical and atomic
quantum systems. Their combination of mechanical stiffness, temperature
stability, electrical insulation, and vacuum-compatible material
properties is complemented by the geometric freedom of additive
manufacturing. Using LCM, application-specific mounting structures,
lightweight geometries, thermal functionality, and interfaces for
optical and electronic components can be incorporated directly into a
monolithic MIOB, which can be manufactured effectively and scalably. In
combination with active micro-integration and qualified adhesive
bonding, the dimensional tolerances of the ceramic printing process are
compatible with the alignment requirements of precision optical systems.

The CerAMRef demonstrates this approach at the subsystem level.
Doppler-free rubidium spectroscopy is realized within an approximately
6~mL, 15~g fiber-coupled module based on a printed Al#sub[2]O#sub[3] optical bench. A
laser stabilized to the reference achieves a fractional frequency
instability of $3 times 10^(- 12)$ at 1~s, demonstrating that
substantial miniaturization can be achieved while retaining performance
suitable for cold-atom and metrology applications.

The ceramic platform is being extended both in terms of materials and
integrated functionality. In addition to Al#sub[2]O#sub[3], ZrO#sub[2] is now processed for
thermally isolating structures such as compact vapor-cell ovens and
micro-optical benches, illustrated by the heated cesium cell of the
compact optically pumped magnetometer presented in #link(<sec:opm>)[4],
where it is intended to reduce the stand-off distance to the sample. The
complete printing, debinding, and sintering process chain for oxide
ceramics is available in-house. Selective metallization is under
development to introduce electrical interconnects and locally integrated
functional elements. The compact OPM, aimed at space-borne
biomagnetometry, and the active optical systems for cold-atom
manipulation presented in
#link(<sec:opm>)[4] and #link(<sec:active_optics>)[5]
further illustrate how the same manufacturing and integration approach
can be transferred from a spectroscopy reference to different
atomic-sensor architectures.

Future developments will increasingly combine mechanical, optical,
thermal, and electrical functionality within the ceramic substrate. Such
multifunctional components can reduce part count and assembly complexity
while providing stable and reproducible interfaces between optical
components, atomic media, and higher-level sensor systems. In parallel,
the platform is also being applied towards compact quantum memory
modules based on a heated atomic vapor cell, extending the
micro-integration approach demonstrated for the CerAMRef and the OPM.
This makes additive manufacturing of technical ceramics a promising
enabling technology for the further transition of atomic quantum devices
from laboratory experiments towards compact systems for field and space
applications.

